 chapter with, so each one must be a flow a newcomer can follow in order, not a
 list of places.
 
+**One tour per outcome, not one per chapter.** Before writing a chapter's
+tours, list the outcomes of its main flow — the branches a real user can end
+up on — and write a tour for each outcome that has its own code path. A login
+chapter is not one tour "from the dialog to the main window"; it is "logging in
+and reaching the main window", "exiting from the login dialog" and "logging
+out and coming back to the dialog", because a reader who only walks the happy
+path will assume the other branches work like it (the first SE2 book had
+exactly this gap: a reader took the exit branch for a retry). Name each tour
+by its outcome. Branches that share code with the happy path except for one
+decision still get their own short tour when the decision is where a newcomer
+would be wrong; a branch that is one line and self-explanatory can instead be
+a single step with a `why` that names it inside the happy-path tour. Retries
+that happen inside a dialog or a loop belong to the tour of the code that
+loops, not to the caller's tour — say where the retry lives.
